            "message": {
              "role": "assistant",
              "content": "When a user types a domain name into a browser, the process begins with a local cache check. If the IP address is not stored locally, the browser queries a recursive resolver, typically provided by the Internet Service Provider. This resolver acts as an intermediary, handling the complex lookup process on behalf of the client. If the resolver has the record cached and the Time-to-Live (TTL) has not expired, it returns the IP address immediately, significantly speeding up the connection.\n\nIf the cache is empty or the TTL has expired, the resolver initiates a hierarchical search. It first queries a root name server, which directs it to the appropriate Top-Level Domain (TLD) server, such as .com or .org. The TLD server then points to the authoritative name server for the specific domain. Unlike recursive servers, which perform the entire lookup chain for the client, authoritative servers only provide answers for domains they directly manage. They do not query other servers.\n\nOnce the authoritative server returns the IP address, the recursive resolver caches this result for the duration specified by the TTL. This caching mechanism reduces network traffic and latency for subsequent requests. Finally, the resolver sends the IP address back to the browser, which establishes a connection to the web server. This entire process ensures efficient, scalable, and reliable internet navigation by balancing speed through caching with accuracy through authoritative data sources."
            },
